(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-nervous-system-motor-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Nervous System: Motor — Physiology Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"What are the three subareas of the motor cortex?","choices":["Broca's area, Wernicke's area, and the angular gyrus","Primary motor cortex, premotor area, and supplemental motor area","Only the primary motor cortex -- subareas exist solely in sensory, not motor, cortex","Occipital, parietal, and cerebellar motor coordination areas"],"answer":1,"exp":"The motor cortex is divided into the primary motor cortex (fine motor movement, unequal topographic representation), the premotor area (movement of muscle groups for a task), and the supplemental motor area (bilateral movements, positional/attitudinal support for fine control). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What deficit results from damage to Broca's area?","choices":["Loss of equilibrium and postural balance control","Complete loss of visual field perception","Decreased speech capability (motor aphasia)","Selective motor apraxia affecting the hands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Damage to Broca's area causes decreased speech capability; a closely associated area controls the appropriate respiratory function needed for speech. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What deficit results from damage to the hand skills area of the motor cortex?","choices":["Complete paralysis of the entire contralateral side of the body","Selective loss of the patellar tendon reflex","Complete loss of auditory perception bilaterally","Motor apraxia — the inability to perform fine hand movements"],"answer":3,"exp":"Damage to the hand skills area of the motor cortex causes motor apraxia, the inability to perform fine hand movements. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"From what three cortical regions does the corticospinal tract originate, and in what approximate proportions?","choices":["Occipital cortex (30%), temporal cortex (30%), and parietal association cortex (40%)","Primary motor cortex (30%), supplementary motor areas (30%), and somatic sensory areas (40%)","Entirely from the primary motor cortex, contributing 100% of corticospinal fibers","Entirely from the cerebellum (100%), with no contribution from the cerebral cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"The corticospinal tract originates from the primary motor cortex (about 30%), supplementary motor areas (about 30%), and somatic sensory areas (about 40%). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are Betz cells, and what fraction of corticospinal fibers do they contribute?","choices":["Giant pyramidal cells that give rise to large, fast-conducting fibers (~70 m/sec), but make up only about 3% of total corticospinal fibers","Small interneurons found within the anterior horn of the spinal cord that make up the majority of corticospinal fibers, conducting signals at only about 15 m/sec","Glial cells that support and myelinate the corticospinal tract, comprising the majority of the tract's total cross-sectional area within the medulla","Sensory relay neurons found only within the thalamus, projecting exclusively to somatosensory rather than motor cortex and contributing no corticospinal fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Betz cells are giant pyramidal cells in the primary motor cortex that give rise to large corticospinal fibers with fast transmission rates (~70 m/sec), but the roughly 34,000 Betz cell fibers make up only about 3% of the total ~1 million corticospinal fibers — the remaining 97% are small-diameter fibers conducting background tonic signals. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Where do most corticospinal fibers cross to the opposite side of the body?","choices":["In the spinal cord at each segmental level, just before exiting to synapse with anterior motor neurons","In the thalamus, prior to descending together in a single uncrossed corticospinal tract","They never cross to the opposite side, descending the entire way in an uncrossed ipsilateral corticospinal tract","In the medulla, descending afterward in the lateral corticospinal tracts"],"answer":3,"exp":"The majority of corticospinal fibers cross to the opposite side in the medulla and then descend in the lateral corticospinal tracts, synapsing with interneurons, anterior motor neurons, and a few sensory relay neurons in the cord's gray matter. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What kind of movement does stimulation of the red nucleus (magnocellular portion) produce, compared to the primary motor cortex?","choices":["No movement at all, since the red nucleus's magnocellular portion functions purely as a sensory relay station with no direct motor output of its own","Movement identical in precision to primary motor cortex stimulation, since the rubrospinal tract is thought to carry an exact duplicate of the corticospinal signal","Relatively fine motor movement, but not as discrete as movement from the primary motor cortex — an accessory route for discrete signal transmission","Only coordinated movements of the eyes, since the rubrospinal tract is believed to project exclusively to the extraocular motor nuclei of the brainstem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stimulation of the red nucleus's magnocellular portion (which receives substantial input from the primary motor cortex and gives rise to the rubrospinal tract) causes relatively fine motor movement, though not as discrete as movement produced by the primary motor cortex — serving as an accessory route for discrete motor signals. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How do the pontine and medullary reticular nuclei differ in their effect on antigravity muscles?","choices":["Neither nucleus affects antigravity muscles directly at all; both are thought to act exclusively through the cerebellum to modulate limb flexor tone during gait","The pontine reticular nuclei excite antigravity muscles; the medullary reticular nuclei inhibit them","The pontine reticular nuclei inhibit antigravity muscles and have low natural excitability; the medullary reticular nuclei excite them and receive collateral input from higher centers","Both nuclei excite antigravity muscles to an equal degree, differing only in the conduction speed of the pontine versus medullary reticulospinal tracts"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pontine reticular nuclei excite antigravity muscles (via the pontine reticulospinal tract) and have high natural excitability; the medullary reticular nuclei inhibit antigravity muscles (via the medullary reticulospinal tract) and receive collateral input from the corticospinal and rubrospinal tracts to counterbalance the pons. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What sensory information does the macula (within the utricle and saccule) detect?","choices":["Angular (rotational) acceleration of the head, detected via the inertial lag of endolymph fluid within the semicircular ducts rather than by gravity","Sound frequency, via mechanically tuned hair cells arranged tonotopically along the basilar membrane rather than within the macula itself","The position of objects within the visual field, via signals relayed from the retina through the vestibular nuclei during head movement","The orientation of the head with respect to gravity, via gravity-sensitive hair cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"The macula, found within the utricle and saccule, is a gravity-sensitive receptor consisting of gravity-sensitive hair cells (made top-heavy by statoconia) that detect the orientation of the head with respect to gravity. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What happens to a vestibular hair cell when its stereocilia bend toward the kinocilium versus away from it?","choices":["Bending in one direction causes depolarization; bending in the opposite direction causes hyperpolarization","Bending of the stereocilia has no electrical effect on hair cells at all; only sustained static pressure changes their resting membrane potential","Hair cells are not directionally sensitive at all, responding with an identical depolarization regardless of which way their stereocilia are bent","Bending in either direction always causes depolarization, with the kinocilium serving only to anchor the bundle rather than set polarity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vestibular hair cells have stereocilia and one large kinocilium that make them directionally sensitive: bending in one direction causes depolarization, while bending in the opposite direction causes hyperpolarization. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do the semicircular ducts detect, and how are they oriented?","choices":["Only vertical head movements confined to the sagittal plane, since the ducts are oriented in parallel with one another rather than at right angles","Linear acceleration and gravity, sensed by gravity-sensitive hair cells oriented within a single horizontal plane inside each of the three ducts","Sound vibration transmitted through the endolymph, with all three ducts on each side oriented parallel to one another to create a tonotopic frequency map","Angular (rotational) acceleration of the head, with all three ducts oriented at 90 degrees to each other representing the three planes in space"],"answer":3,"exp":"The semicircular ducts detect angular (rotational) acceleration of the head; all three ducts on each side are oriented at 90 degrees to each other, representing all three planes in space, with an ampulla (containing the crista ampullaris) at the end of each duct. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How does rotation of a semicircular duct activate its sensory receptors?","choices":["The bony duct wall itself directly senses rotation through embedded stretch receptors, entirely without any relative movement of the endolymph fluid inside it","Endolymph tends to remain stationary due to inertia while the duct rotates around it, causing relative movement of endolymph that activates receptors in the crista ampullaris","Rotation causes the duct itself to physically shrink and compress the crista ampullaris, directly triggering receptor activation without fluid involvement","Perilymph, not endolymph, provides the sensory signal by lagging behind the rotating duct wall and displacing the crista ampullaris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because endolymph tends to remain stationary due to inertia, rotation of the duct in one direction causes relative movement of the endolymph in the opposite direction, activating receptors in the crista ampullaris; stopping rotation produces the opposite effect. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the \"predictive\" function of the semicircular ducts in maintaining equilibrium?","choices":["They predict situations in which equilibrium will be affected, allowing corrective measures to be initiated before equilibrium is actually lost","They have no predictive function at all, serving only to passively confirm equilibrium loss after it has already occurred, well after corrective reflexes begin","They only respond after equilibrium has already been fully lost, sending corrective signals to the brain only once an actual fall has already begun","They only function during sleep, becoming active specifically when neck proprioceptors and visual input are unavailable to help maintain equilibrium"],"answer":0,"exp":"The semicircular ducts predict situations in which equilibrium will be affected, sending this information to the brain so that corrective measures can be initiated before equilibrium is actually affected; neck proprioceptors and visual input also contribute. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the general function of the cerebellum in motor control?","choices":["It only functions during periods of sleep, generating the rhythmic motor patterns responsible for eye movements and muscle twitches seen during REM","It initiates all voluntary movement entirely independently, with the motor cortex serving only a minor supporting role once a movement has been planned there","It has no role in movement coordination whatsoever, functioning instead purely as a site for long-term procedural memory storage and consolidation","It coordinates and sequences muscle activity, monitoring and making corrective adjustments by comparing actual motor movements with intended movements"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cerebellum (\"little brain\") coordinates muscle activity, sequences motor activities, and monitors/makes corrective adjustments to movements initiated by other parts of the brain by comparing actual motor movements with intended movements. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which functional zone of the cerebellum is responsible for the sequencing, timing, and coordination of movement?","choices":["None of these zones is involved in timing","The intermediate zone","The lateral zone","The vermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cerebellum is functionally organized along its longitudinal axis: the vermis (center) controls axial movements of neck/shoulders/hips, the intermediate zone controls motion of distal limbs (especially hands/feet), and the lateral zone controls sequencing, timing, and coordination of movement. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What pattern of signaling do deep cerebellar nuclear cells produce at the beginning of a motion, and what function does this serve?","choices":["Only continuous inhibitory signals throughout the entire motion, suppressing the movement steadily from initiation until the intended target is finally reached","Excitatory signals to enhance movement, followed milliseconds later by inhibitory signals, providing a damping function to prevent overshoot (like a delay-line circuit)","Only excitatory signals with no damping component, continuing to accelerate the movement throughout its full course with no braking mechanism at all","Random, unpatterned signals bearing no consistent timing relationship to the onset, progression, or completion of the intended motion"],"answer":1,"exp":"At the beginning of motion, deep cerebellar nuclear cells send excitatory signals into the motor pathway to enhance movement, followed milliseconds later by inhibitory signals — providing a damping function that prevents the movement from overshooting its mark, resembling a delay-line negative feedback circuit. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What role does climbing fiber input play in Purkinje cell function?","choices":["It has no lasting effect on Purkinje cells at all, producing only a brief, immediately reversible depolarization with no change to their future sensitivity","It only affects the inferior olivary complex itself, feeding back locally without ever reaching or altering Purkinje cell activity in the cerebellar cortex","It adjusts the long-term sensitivity of Purkinje cells to mossy fiber input (from muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organ, proprioceptors), adjusting the feedback control of muscle movement","It directly innervates skeletal muscle fibers itself, bypassing Purkinje cells and the deep cerebellar nuclei entirely to produce contraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Climbing fiber input adjusts the sensitivity of Purkinje cells to stimulation by parallel fibers, changing the long-term sensitivity of Purkinje cells to mossy fiber input (from muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organ, and proprioceptors) — adjusting the feedback control of muscle movement, since precise motor movement must be learned. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the function of the inferior olivary complex in correcting motor errors?","choices":["It compares the intent of movement (from the corticospinal tract and brainstem motor centers) with the actual movement that occurred (via sensory feedback), altering climbing fiber output to the cerebellum to correct any mismatch","It has no role in motor error correction at all, functioning instead purely as a relay station for auditory input on its way to the thalamus","It only processes visual information, comparing retinal input with expected visual flow patterns to help stabilize gaze during head movement","It directly controls the basal ganglia with no cerebellar involvement at all, sending its motor error signals straight to the striatum instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior olivary complex receives input from the corticospinal tract and brainstem motor centers (representing intended movement) as well as sensory information about actual movement; if a mismatch occurs, its output to the cerebellum via climbing fibers is altered to correct the mismatch. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the specific role of the lateral cerebellar hemisphere in motor planning?","choices":["It has no role in motor planning whatsoever, functioning instead exclusively to maintain equilibrium through direct connections to the vestibular nuclei","It communicates with the premotor/sensory cortex and basal ganglia, receiving the movement plan and timing the sequential events to carry it out","It directly generates the plan for movement entirely on its own, with no input required from the premotor cortex, sensory cortex, or basal ganglia","It only processes auditory information, relaying cochlear input to the inferior colliculus rather than participating in any motor timing function"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral cerebellar hemisphere is responsible for the planning and timing of sequential movements — it communicates with the premotor and sensory cortex and the corresponding area of the basal ganglia (where the movement plan originates), receiving the plan and timing the sequential events to carry it out. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What are the four nuclei that make up the basal ganglia?","choices":["Thalamus, hypothalamus, hippocampus, and amygdala -- the four interconnected limbic structures classically grouped together as the basal ganglia","Striatum (caudate and putamen), globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and subthalamus","Frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital association cortex, the four cortical regions that together form the functional basal ganglia","Cerebellum, pons, medulla, and midbrain -- the four brainstem structures traditionally grouped together to form the basal ganglia"],"answer":1,"exp":"The basal ganglia consist of four nuclei: the striatum (caudate and putamen), globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and subthalamus — the principal subcortical components of parallel circuits linking the thalamus with the cerebral cortex. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What kinds of complex motor activities does the basal ganglia help control?","choices":["Only coordinated movements of the eyes, including saccadic jumps and the smooth pursuit tracking of a moving visual target","Complex patterns of motor activity such as writing, using scissors, throwing balls, shoveling dirt, and aspects of vocalization","Only cardiac and respiratory rhythms, setting the basal pacemaker rate for both the heart and the brainstem's respiratory control centers","Only simple reflexive movements like the patellar or withdrawal reflex, with no involvement in any voluntary or learned motor sequence"],"answer":1,"exp":"The basal ganglia control complex patterns of motor activity, including writing, using scissors, throwing balls, shoveling dirt, and some aspects of vocalization — thought to function in the timing, scaling, and initiation of motion. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is the proposed role of the caudate circuit, given its extensive input from cortical association areas?","choices":["A role in the cognitive control of motor functions, since most motor actions occur as a result of a sequence of thoughts","It has no connections to the rest of the basal ganglia at all, receiving cortical input directly and projecting straight back to that same cortical area","Direct control of reflexive spinal withdrawal only, bypassing the cortex entirely to synapse directly onto spinal cord interneurons","Exclusively controls eye movements, receiving input only from the visual cortex rather than from the broader cortical association areas"],"answer":0,"exp":"The caudate circuit receives large input from association areas of the cortex and projects mostly to the globus pallidus; because most motor actions arise from a sequence of thoughts, the caudate circuit is thought to play a role in the cognitive control of motor functions. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the proposed role of the putamen circuit?","choices":["A role in the execution of motor movement, via connections mostly from premotor and supplemental motor cortex, through the putamen, and back to the motor cortex","It solely mediates emotional processing, receiving input exclusively from the amygdala and cingulate cortex rather than from any motor cortical area","It plays no role in motor function at all, functioning instead purely as a relay station for somatosensory information heading to the thalamus","It is responsible for producing speech only, connecting exclusively to Broca's area rather than to the premotor or supplemental motor cortex"],"answer":0,"exp":"The putamen circuit runs mostly from the premotor and supplemental motor cortex to the putamen and back to the motor cortex, and is thought to play a role in the execution of motor movement (as opposed to the caudate circuit's more cognitive role). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is the primary role of the cortical level in the integration of motor control, according to this lecture?","choices":["Maintaining equilibrium by adjusting axial and limb muscle tone alone, a role handled by the brainstem level rather than any cortical involvement","Directly contracting individual muscle fibers with no intermediary circuitry, bypassing the spinal cord's preprogrammed pattern generators entirely","Issuing commands that set into motion patterns already available in the spinal cord, controlling their intensity and modifying timing","Preprogramming basic patterns of movement like withdrawal reflexes, a function that instead belongs entirely to the spinal cord level of integration"],"answer":2,"exp":"At the cortical level of motor integration, the cortex issues commands that set into motion patterns of movement already available in the spinal cord, controlling the intensity of the movement and modifying its timing — distinct from the spinal cord level (preprogrammed patterns) and brainstem level (equilibrium via axial tone). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"According to the overall scheme of motor integration, what dual role do the basal ganglia play?","choices":["They assist the cortex in executing subconscious but learned patterns of movement, and help plan sequential patterns to accomplish a purposeful task","They only inhibit all movement globally, acting purely as a brake mechanism with no independent role in planning purposeful sequences of motion","They have no functional role in motor integration at all, serving instead purely as a relay for olfactory and limbic signals to the cortex","They function only at the spinal cord level, integrating reflex circuitry rather than assisting the cortex in learned, subconscious movement patterns"],"answer":0,"exp":"In the overall integration of motor function, the basal ganglia assist the cortex in executing subconscious but learned patterns of movement, and also help plan sequential patterns of movement needed to accomplish a purposeful task. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"From which thalamic nuclei does the motor cortex receive input important for coordinating function between the motor cortex, basal ganglia, and cerebellum?","choices":["The ventrolateral and ventroanterior nuclei of the thalamus","The ventrobasal complex of the thalamus only","The pulvinar nucleus of the thalamus","The lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ventrolateral and ventroanterior nuclei of the thalamus provide input to the motor cortex important for coordinating function between the motor cortex, basal ganglia, and cerebellum, while the intralaminar nuclei help control the level of excitability of the motor cortex. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Approximately how many giant pyramidal (Betz) cells must be excited simultaneously to cause muscle contraction?","choices":["All 34,000 Betz cells at once","1-2","10,000","50-100"],"answer":3,"exp":"Excitation of approximately 50-100 giant pyramidal (Betz) cells is needed to cause muscle contraction; most corticospinal fibers synapse with interneurons, though some synapse directly with alpha motor neurons, especially those innervating the fingers and hand in the cervical enlargement. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How is the brainstem functionally related to the spinal cord?","choices":["It exclusively controls voluntary fine motor movement of the hands and fingers, a function that is actually performed by the primary motor cortex rather than the brainstem","It only processes visual information relayed from the retina, forwarding it to the occipital cortex with no involvement in motor or sensory function for the head","It acts as an extension of the spinal cord, performing similar motor and sensory functions for the face and head via the cranial nerves, and contains centers for stereotypic movement and equilibrium","It has no functional relationship to the spinal cord at all, operating instead as an entirely separate and independently controlled neural structure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The brainstem functions as an extension of the spinal cord, performing motor and sensory functions for the face and head (via the cranial nerves) similar to how the spinal cord functions for the body below the head, and it contains centers for stereotypic movement and equilibrium. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What role does sensory feedback (from muscle spindles, tactile receptors, and proprioceptors) play in motor control?","choices":["It plays no role once a movement has begun, since the initial motor cortex command is thought to fully specify the entire trajectory in advance","It fine-tunes muscle movement, allowing auto-correction for length mismatch in the spindle and informing the motor cortex of the effectiveness of an intended action","It only matters for spinal reflexes, never contributing meaningfully to voluntary movement once the motor cortex has issued its initial command","It is only relevant to cerebellar function, playing no role at all in informing the motor cortex about the effectiveness of an intended action"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sensory feedback from muscle spindles, tactile receptors, and proprioceptors fine-tunes muscle movement — a length mismatch detected by the spindle causes auto-correction, and skin compression provides feedback to the motor cortex on how effective an intended action has been. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Besides the direct corticospinal pathway, what indirect pathways carry signals from the motor cortex?","choices":["Signals to the basal ganglia, cerebellum, and brainstem nuclei","Signals only to the auditory cortex and inferior colliculus","Signals only to the retina and visual cortex","Only one output pathway exists -- the corticospinal tract"],"answer":0,"exp":"Besides the direct pathway (corticospinal tract, for discrete detailed movement), the motor cortex sends signals via indirect pathways to the basal ganglia, cerebellum, and brainstem nuclei. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
